{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"loathsome-depressed-curassow","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# Give the completion verifier budgets that scale with the ticket\n\n## Context / Problem\n\n`d59e` removed the context wall (24,000 → 100,000 chars, nothing elided) and fixed the false\n\"install the agents extra\" remedy. It deliberately did NOT fix the reason ticket\n`9fd4-a94c-156e-4a56` still cannot close: the verifier's remaining budgets are flat constants that\ndo not scale with the number of acceptance criteria, even though the work — and the verdict the\ngate must produce — is linear in that count.\n\nThe intent for this verifier is a MAXIMAL budget on context, output and tool use, bounded only\nenough to stop a runaway loop that spends tool calls without producing results. It currently has\nneither: the budgets are too small for a large ticket AND they do not distinguish a large job from\na runaway.\n\nThis epic is the follow-up. It should be BRAINSTORMED before decomposition — the adversarial review\nthat produced these findings also invalidated three plausible-looking designs, so the shape is not\nyet settled.\n\n## What is already measured (do not re-derive)\n\n1. **The primary step budget is flat.** `completion.py:337-338` applies\n   `max(cfg.max_iterations, _VERIFY_MIN_STEPS=480)` regardless of criteria count. 9fd4 has 14\n   criteria (~34 steps each) and exhausted 480 after ~780s ≈ 3.25s per model request. A naive\n   `34 × 14` scaling lands back at ~480 — the budget that already failed — so roughly 70-100 steps\n   per criterion is the floor worth testing.\n2. **A step-budget exhaustion is a dead end.** The runner's `UsageLimitExceeded` arm raises\n   `LLMRunnerError` (`runner.py:465`, `raise budget_err from exc`), which is NOT an\n   `UnretryableOutputError`, so it falls through recovery's `except UnretryableOutputError`\n   (`completion_recovery.py:405`) and never reaches the fallback.\n3. **The obvious discriminant does not work.** `runner.py:414-417` sets\n   `request_limit = ceil(max_iter/2)` and `tool_calls_limit = max(8, max_iter)` — 240 vs 480 at the\n   default. A classic runaway (one tool call per model turn) trips `request_limit` FIRST, so\n   `failure.py:199-205`'s request-limit-vs-tool-calls-limit split does NOT separate a runaway from\n   a large job in the direction one would expect. Any design resting on that split is unsound.\n4. **The evidence bounds are incoherent above 8 criteria.** `_EVIDENCE_CHARS` (12,000 per\n   criterion) vs `_MAX_TOTAL_EVIDENCE_CHARS` (96,000 aggregate) means the affordable mean is\n   96,000/N — 6,857 for 9fd4's 14 criteria — the same bound-vs-bound incoherence `d59e` fixed for\n   criteria-vs-context. Worse, the aggregate check runs INSIDE the loop AFTER each billable call,\n   so a 14-criterion ticket pays for all 14 evidence calls and then hard-fails with no verdict.\n5. **Recovery is a weaker oracle than the aggregate path.** Its finalizer decides `met` from\n   bounded evidence SUMMARIES, never raw source, and `_validate_coverage` enforces coverage SHAPE,\n   not truth. Availability must not be bought by routing more traffic to it.\n6. **Recovery's per-criterion re-send is uncached.** The `cache_control` breakpoint is on the\n   `system_prompt` block (`runner.py:330-338`), but the ticket context is passed in `instructions`,\n   so each per-criterion call re-bills the whole context (14 × 8,570 ≈ 120k tokens for 9fd4).\n   Moving the invariant context into the cached block is a real saving — but it would put UNTRUSTED\n   ticket text into the highest-authority prompt block, which `completion_verifier.md:29-36`\n   explicitly says arrives \"in the user message\". That trade needs deciding, not assuming.\n7. **There is no wall-clock deadline anywhere.** `DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_S` is per-request only; no\n   SIGALRM/deadline exists in the LLM path. So step scaling is not silently neutered — but a\n   30-criterion ticket at 70 steps/criterion implies ~57 minutes of a blocking `rebar transition`\n   with no progress output.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] ticket `9fd4-a94c-156e-4a56` closes at DEFAULT settings, with no `REBAR_LLM_MAX_STEPS`\n      override and no `--force-close`, with the command output recorded as evidence\n- [ ] the verifier's step and output budgets scale with the criteria count and never fall below\n      today's values, so no ticket that verifies now loses budget\n- [ ] a step-budget exhaustion of the primary verifier no longer terminates the close, and the\n      chosen entry mechanism is justified against finding (3) — a design resting on the\n      request-limit/tool-calls-limit split is rejected unless it demonstrably handles the\n      one-tool-call-per-turn runaway\n- [ ] the evidence bounds are made mutually coherent for the full supported criteria range, and an\n      over-budget run is detected BEFORE paying for the evidence calls rather than after\n- [ ] a runaway loop is still stopped, with the cost bounded and stated, and a test demonstrates\n      the stop\n- [ ] a long verification reports progress and/or is bounded by a stated wall-clock ceiling, so a\n      close cannot block for an hour with no output\n- [ ] no change routes additional traffic to the recovery path without an explicit statement of\n      why the fidelity loss in finding (5) is acceptable there","id":"94b4-059e-a74a-4cf8","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"epic","title":"Give the completion verifier budgets that scale with the ticket"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785451254852570001,"uuid":"52386c83-d2aa-4fe8-8b1d-a7b7ce757e76"}